    <statement><p>Calculate the derivative of the function below using the quotient rule.</p>
<p><me>y= \frac{6x}{9x+2}</me></p>
<p><m>f(x) =</m>  <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0001"/> and <m>g(x)=</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>which means that</p>
<p><m>f'(x) =</m>  <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0003"/> and <m>g'(x)=</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p>And thus <me>\left[\frac{6x}{9x+2}\right]' = \frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</me>
where <m>p(x) =</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0005"/> and <m>q(x) =</m><fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0006"/></p>
</statement>
    <hint><p><m>y</m> is a quotient of two functions, <m>6x</m> and <m>9x+2</m>.</p>
<p>The quotient rule says that <m>\displaystyle \left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right]'=\frac{g(x)f'(x)-f(x)g'(x)}{\left[g(x)\right]^2}</m>.</p>
</hint>
