    <solution><p>The function <m>\displaystyle y=\frac{6x}{9x+2}</m> is a quotient of two functions, say <m>f(x)= 6x</m> and <m>g(x)=9x+2</m>.  Then taking the derivatives we get <m>f'(x)=6</m> and <m>g'(x)=9</m>.  Finally, the quotient rule says that 
<me>\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right]'=\frac{g(x)f'(x)-f(x)g'(x)}{\left[g(x)\right]^2}</me>
so <me>y'= \frac{(9x+2) \cdot (6)  - (6x)\cdot (9)}{(9x+2)^2}= \frac{12}{(9 x + 2)^2}.</me></p>
</solution>
